== Transfer <sec:transfer>
#idx("transfer", target: [@sec:transfer])

#source(63)In this section, we see that the transfer map $Tr_(H, G)$ which we
investigated in Section~3.6 of Volume~I for ordinary cohomology has an analogue
for any generalised cohomology theory. The reader should be warned that unlike
in ordinary cohomology (Proposition~3.6.17 of Volume~I), in general $res_(G, H)$
followed by $Tr_(H, G)$ is not multiplication by $|G : H|$.

Generalised cohomology theories are stable under suspension in the sense that
$tilde(h)^r (X) tilde.equiv tilde(h)^(r+1) (S X)$, and in the next section, we
interpret the transfer as a "stable map" from $B H$ to $B G$. We shall discuss
(without proof) the Segal conjecture, which is essentially the statement that
all stable maps between classifying spaces are generated by the transfers and
the group homomorphisms.

For any covering map $p : X -> B$ of CW-complexes with finite fibres of size
$n$, and any generalised cohomology theory $h^*$, we define a transfer map
$p_! : h^* (X) -> h^* (B)$. In case $H$ is a subgroup of a group $G$ of finite
index, we saw in Theorem~@th:classifying-fibrations that there is a covering map
$B H -> B G$ with fibres $G slash H$. In this case, and taking $h^*$ to be
ordinary cohomology theory $H^*$, we obtain the transfer map $Tr_(H, G)$ which
we defined in Section~3.6 of Volume~I.

We define the map $p_!$ in two stages. First we define a *pretransfer map*#idx(
  "pretransfer map",
)#idx("map", "pretransfer")
$ P upright(T r) : B -> E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) X^n $
#idx("$P upright(T r)$")as follows. We set
$
  macron(X) = {(x_1, dots, x_n) in X^n | {x_1, dots, x_n} "is a fibre of" p}.
$
There is an obvious covering map $macron(X) -> B$ with fibres of size $n!$, and
a free action of $Sigma_n$, so that $macron(X) -> B$ is the principal
$Sigma_n$-bundle corresponding to $X -> B$. It follows that there is a
classifying map $B -> B Sigma_n$ so that $macron(X) -> B$ is the pullback
$ #ordered-fibres $
The maps $macron(X) arrow.r.hook X^n$ and $macron(X) -> E Sigma_n$ give a map
$macron(X) -> E Sigma_n times X^n$ and hence, by quotienting out the action of
$Sigma_n$ on both sides, a map $B -> E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) X^n$ which is the
required pretransfer map.

The second stage of the construction of $p_!$ is the Dyer–Lashof map#idx(
  "Dyer–Lashof map",
)#idx("map", "Dyer–Lashof"). The representability theorem for cohomology
theories (see for example Switzer [@bib:Switzer1973], Chapter~9) says that for
CW-complexes $X$, $tilde(h)^r (X) = [X; K_r]$ where $K_r$ are CW-complexes with
homotopy equivalences $K_r tilde.eq Omega K_(r+1)$. In fact, we shall not need
to have recourse to this theorem in this book, since we give explicit spaces
$K_r$ every time we introduce a generalised cohomology theory. For example if
$tilde(h)^r (X) = tilde(H)^r (X; pi)$ then $K_r = K(pi, r)$. Note also that
conversely, given such spaces $K_r$, the definition $tilde(h)^r (X) = [X; K_r]$
defines a #source(64)reduced generalised cohomology theory. The corresponding
unreduced theory is $h^r (X) = [X_+; K_r]$. The Dyer–Lashof map is a certain map
$ D L : E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) (K_r)^n -> K_r $
#idx("$D L$")built using the fact that $K_r$ is homotopic to the $k$-fold loop
space on $K_(r+k)$ for all $k$. Now if $alpha in h^r (X)$ is represented by a
map $hat(alpha) : X_+ -> K_r$, then the transfer $p_! (alpha) in h^r (B)$ is
represented by the composite map
$
  B_+ limits(arrow.r.long)^(P upright(T r))
  (E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) X^n)_+
  limits(arrow.r.long)^(1 times hat(alpha)^n)
  E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) (K_r)^n limits(arrow.r.long)^(D L) K_r.
$

It remains to define the Dyer–Lashof map $D L$ [@bib:Dyer1962]. To define this,
we use a specific model for $E Sigma_n$, called the space of *little cubes*#idx(
  "little cubes",
). Boardman and Vogt [@bib:Boardman1968] define the space $cal(C)_k (n)$#idx(
  "$cal(C)_k (n)$",
) of little cubes as follows. Let $I^k$ be a $k$ dimensional cube, and identify
the sphere $S^k$ with $I^k slash dot(I)^k$. Then $cal(C)_k (n)$ is the space of
maps
$ S^k -> or.big_(j=1)^n S^k $
having the property that there are $n$ disjoint subcubes $I_j^k$ of the form
$[a_1, b_1] times dots.c times [a_k, b_k]$ inside the left hand copy of $S^k$,
such that $I_j^k$ is mapped by the linear map
$
  (x_1, dots, x_k) |-> scaled(
    ((x_1 - a_1)/(b_1 - a_1), dots,
      (x_k - a_k)/(b_k - a_k))
  )
$
to the $j$th sphere on the right, and points outside these subcubes are sent to
the basepoint. We topologise $cal(C)_k (n)$ as a subspace of the space of maps.
The group $Sigma_n$ acts freely by permuting the $n$ little cubes $I_j^k$.

#lemma[
  $cal(C)_k (n)$ is $(k - 2)$-connected.
] <lem:little-cubes-connected>

#proof[
  There is an obvious fibration $cal(C)_k (n) -> cal(C)_k (n - 1)$ whose fibre
  is the space of subcubes
  $I_n^k subset.eq I^k without union.big_(j=1)^(n-1) I_j^k$. Replacing such a
  subcube by its centre is a homotopy equivalence of this space with
  $I^k without union.big_(j=1)^(n-1) I_j^k$, and this space is homotopy
  equivalent to $I^k$ with $(n - 1)$ points removed. So this fibre is
  $(k - 2)$-connected, and the lemma follows by induction on $n$ using the long
  exact sequence of homotopy groups of a fibration
  (Theorem~@th:fibration-long-exact-sequence).
]

Now there are obvious inclusions $cal(C)_k (n) arrow.r.hook cal(C)_(k+1) (n)$,
and we define
$ cal(C)(n) = union.big_(k=1)^infinity cal(C)_k (n) $
with the weak topology with respect to the union.

#proposition[
  $cal(C)(n)$ is a contractible CW-complex on which $Sigma_n$ acts freely.
] <prop:little-cubes-contractible>

#proof[
  #source(65)Since $cal(C)(n)$ was given the weak topology with respect to the
  union, it is a CW-complex. By the lemma, we have $pi_i (cal(C)(n)) = 0$ for
  all $i gt.eq 0$, and so by Theorem~@th:whitehead-cw, $cal(C)(n)$ is
  contractible.
]

It follows that we may use $cal(C)(n)$ as our model for $E Sigma_n$, and
$cal(C)(n) slash Sigma_n = B Sigma_n$#idx("$B Sigma_n$").

Now suppose $K_r tilde.eq Omega^k K_(r+k) = Map_* (S^k, s_0; K_(r+k), x_0)$ as
above. Then composition of maps gives a well defined map
$
  cal(C)_k (n) times_(Sigma_n) (Omega^k K_(r+k))^n -> Omega^k K_(r+k)
$
and hence a map
$ cal(C)_k (n) times_(Sigma_n) (K_r)^n -> K_r $
well defined up to homotopy. Passing to the limit, this gives a map
$ E Sigma_n times_(Sigma_n) (K_r)^n limits(arrow.r.long)^(D L) K_r $
well defined up to homotopy, and this is the Dyer–Lashof map.

This completes the description of the transfer for finite coverings. In fact,
there is a generalisation due to Becker and Gottlieb [@bib:Becker1975]#idx(
  "Becker–Gottlieb transfer",
)#idx("transfer", "Becker–Gottlieb"), for bundles with compact fibre and
structure group a compact Lie group. We now describe a version of their
construction, modified to look as much like the above discussion as possible.

Let $G$ be a compact Lie group and $F$ a compact smooth manifold on which $G$
acts smoothly and faithfully. Let $xi = (p : E -> B)$ be a principal $G$-bundle
and $xi[F] = (p_F : X = E times_G F -> B)$ the associated bundle with structure
group $G$ and fibre $F$ (see Definition~@def:associated-bundle). According to
Definition~@def:universal-bundle (and Theorem~@th:milnor-universal) there is a
map $f : B -> B G$ with $f^* (xi_G) = xi$, so that we have a pullback square
$ #classifying-square $
Also, the obvious map $E times F -> X$ gives us a map
$ E -> Map(F, X) = Map_* (F_+, X), $
where $F_+$ denotes $F$ with disjoint basepoint. These maps give us a map
$E -> E G times Map_* (F_+, X)$, and hence by quotienting out the action of $G$
on both sides, a map
$ P upright(T r) : B -> E G times_G Map_* (F_+, X) $
which is the appropriate pretransfer map for this situation.

As before, to define the Dyer–Lashof map
$ D L : E G times_G Map_* (F_+, K_r) -> K_r $
#source(66)we need a specific model for $E G$. By a theorem of Mostow
[@bib:Mostow1957], we may choose a fixed smooth embedding
$i : F arrow.r.hook V = RR^m$ of $F$ into a finite dimensional real orthogonal
representation $V$ of $G$, so that the $G$-action of $F$ is compatible with the
inclusion. We use as our model for $E G$ the set of embeddings of $F$ in
$RR^infinity$ of the form
$ F limits(->)^i V = RR^m -> RR^infinity $
where this last map is an injective linear map. In other words, we have embedded
$G$ in a real orthogonal group $O(m)$ and used the Grassmannian model for
$E O(m)$ as our model for $E G$. In particular it is clear that this $E G$ is a
contractible space on which $G$ acts freely.

Now choose $epsilon > 0$ small enough so that in an $epsilon$-neighbourhood of
$F$ in $V$, every point has a unique closest point in $F$, so that this
neighbourhood is locally a product of $F$ and a small ball. Such an $epsilon$
exists because $F$ is compact, and the resulting neighbourhood is called a
*tubular neighbourhood*#idx("tubular neighbourhood")#idx(
  "neighbourhood, tubular",
) of $F$ in $V$.

Any element $gamma in E G$ can be regarded as a map
$F arrow.r.hook RR^k subset.eq RR^infinity$ for $k$ large enough, where $RR^k$
is the span of the first $k$ coordinates in $RR^infinity$. We denote by
$F_epsilon$ the corresponding tubular neighbourhood of $F$ in $RR^k$. Denote by
$D_epsilon^k$ an (abstract) open ball of real dimension $k$ and diameter
$epsilon$. Then there is a map
$
  tilde(gamma) : S^k -> (F times D_epsilon^k) union {infinity} = F_+ and S^k
$
defined as follows. We regard the left hand $S^k$ as $RR^k union {infinity}$.
Then $tilde(gamma)$ sends $infinity$, together with all points in $RR^k$ not in
$F_epsilon$, to $infinity$. It sends a point in $F_epsilon$ to the pair
consisting of the closest point in $F$ and the displacement vector (expressing
the displacement from this closest point) in $D_epsilon^k$.

Now suppose $K_r tilde.eq Omega^k K_(r+k)$. Then we have
$
  Map_* (F_+, K_r) tilde.equiv Map_* (F_+ and S^k, K_(r+k)) \
  beta |-> tilde(beta).
$
The map sending a pair of elements $gamma in E G$ and $beta in Map_* (F_+, K_r)$
to the composite
$tilde(beta) compose tilde(gamma) in Map_* (S^k, K_(r+k)) = K_r$ gives us a map
$ D L : E G times_G Map_* (F_+, K_r) -> K_r, $
and this is the Dyer–Lashof map.

Just as before, if $alpha in h^r (X)$ is represented by
$hat(alpha) : X_+ -> K_r$, then the transfer $(p_F)_! (alpha) in h^r (B)$ (or
just $Tr(alpha)$) is represented by the composite map
$
  B_+ limits(arrow.r.long)^(P upright(T r))
  (E G times_G Map_* (F_+, X))_+
  limits(arrow.r.long)^(1 times hat(alpha)_*)
  E G times_G Map_* (F_+, K_r) limits(arrow.r.long)^(D L) K_r.
$

Note that if $F arrow.r.hook V'$ is another embedding as in Mostow's theorem,
then there is a one parameter family of embeddings $F arrow.r.hook V plus.o V'$,
and so the corresponding Dyer–Lashof maps are homotopic.

#source(67)It is clear from the construction of the transfer that it is natural,
in the sense that if
$ #transfer-naturality $
is a pullback of fibrations with fibre $F$ and structure group $G$, then for
$alpha in h^r (X)$ we have
$ Tr mu^* (alpha) = lambda^* Tr(alpha) in h^r (B'). $

#lemma[
  Suppose $tilde(gamma) : S^k -> F_+ and S^k$ represents $gamma in E G$ as
  above, and denote by $pi$ the second projection $F_+ and S^k -> S^k$. Then the
  composite $pi compose tilde(gamma) : S^k -> S^k$ has degree equal to the Euler
  characteristic $chi(F) = sum_(i gt.eq 0) (-1)^i dim H^i (F)$#idx(
    "Euler characteristic",
  ).
] <lem:euler-characteristic-degree>

#proof[
  We first remark that this is clear for the original case where $F$ was a
  finite set of size $n = chi(F)$, since each component of the map
  $S^k -> or.big_(j=1)^n S^k$ has degree one and we are just adding.

  We shall make the general case look like the finite case by using a little
  elementary Morse theory#idx("Morse theory"). If you read Milnor
  [@bib:Milnor1963] up to the end of p.~36, you will know enough Morse theory to
  understand this proof.

  Choose a Morse function $phi.alt : F -> RR$; i.e., a smooth function with
  isolated critical points. Morse theory says that $F$ is homotopy equivalent to
  a CW-complex with one $d$-cell for each critical point of index $d$. Since $F$
  is compact, there are only finitely many critical points. By scaling up the
  Morse function if necessary, we may assume that the neighbourhoods
  $abs(grad phi.alt) < epsilon$ ($epsilon$ as above) of the critical points are
  disjoint open balls in $F$.

  Now the map $pi compose tilde(gamma)$ is really just the displacement map
  $S^k -> D_epsilon^k union {infinity} = S^k$. This displacement map is
  perpendicular to $grad phi.alt$ at the closest point on $F$, and so we can
  just add these to give a map $pi compose tilde(gamma) + grad phi.alt$, which
  we regard as a map from $S^k$ to $D_epsilon^k union {infinity}$. It is
  homotopic to $pi compose tilde(gamma)$ by the homotopy
  $pi compose tilde(gamma) + t grad phi.alt$
  ($0 lt.eq t lt.eq 1$), and therefore has the same degree. But now
  $pi compose tilde(gamma) + grad phi.alt$ sends everything to ${infinity}$
  except for non-overlapping spherical neighbourhoods of the critical points.
  The degree of the map on this spherical neighbourhood is $(-1)^d$ if the index
  is $d$ (Milnor [@bib:Milnor1963], bottom of p.~36). So the total degree of the
  map $S^k -> S^k$ is the sum over the cells in the CW-decomposition of $F$ of
  $(-1)$ to the power of the dimension of the cell; this is the Euler
  characteristic $chi(F)$.
]

#proposition[
  The restriction map followed by the transfer
  $
    H^* (B) limits(arrow.r.long)^(p_F^*) H^* (X) limits(arrow.r.long)^Tr
    H^* (B)
  $
  is equal to multiplication by $chi(F)$.
] <prop:restriction-transfer>

#proof[
  #source(68)By the lemma, if $F -> *$ is a trivial bundle with fibre $F$ over a
  point, then the transfer of the identity element in $H^0 (F)$ is $chi(F)$
  times the identity in $H^0 (*)$.

  Without loss of generality, we may suppose $B$ is connected. Using naturality
  of the transfer on the square
  $ #fibre-square $
  we see that the transfer of the identity element in $H^0 (X)$ is $chi(F)$
  times the identity element in $H^0 (B)$. Now using naturality of the transfer
  on the square
  $ #graph-square $
  for $alpha in H^* (B)$ we have
  $
    Tr p_F^* (alpha) = Tr (p_F, 1)^* (alpha times 1)
    = Delta^* Tr(alpha times 1) = Delta^* (alpha times chi(F)) = chi(F).alpha.
  $
]
